# 1. Professional Practice and Ethics (32). NCMHCE content outline, revised Oct 8, 2025.
CAT = "ncmhceEthics"; SEG = "ETH"
Q = [
("At intake, a counselor explains confidentiality. Which limit must the client hear before sharing anything?",
 "Serious, foreseeable harm", ["Late or missed payments", "Content of dreams shared", "Use of humor in sessions"],
 "ACA B.1.d asks counselors to explain the limits of confidentiality at the start. Serious and foreseeable harm (B.2.a) is the core exception; the other topics are not limits on confidentiality."),
("A client asks to friend her counselor on a personal social media account. The counselor should:",
 "Decline and explain why", ["Accept after discharge only", "Accept but never post", "Ignore the request quietly"],
 "ACA A.5.e bars personal virtual relationships with current clients. Explaining the social media policy keeps the boundary and the alliance; ignoring the request leaves the client guessing."),
("A former client asks for a dinner date three years after counseling ended. Under the ACA Code:",
 "The counselor must decline", ["It is allowed with consultation", "It is allowed if no harm shown", "It needs written consent only"],
 "ACA A.5.c prohibits sexual or romantic relationships with former clients for five years after the last professional contact, so three years is still within the ban."),
("A newly licensed counselor is asked to treat an eating disorder she has never studied. The best response is:",
 "Refer, or train under supervision", ["Accept and learn as she goes", "Accept if the client agrees to it", "Decline all further contact"],
 "ACA C.2.a and C.2.b limit practice to areas of competence and allow new specialties only with education, training and supervised experience. Declining all contact abandons the client."),
("A client signs a release so her physician may receive updates. What should the counselor send?",
 "Only what the release covers", ["The full clinical record", "Session notes verbatim", "Whatever the doctor asks for"],
 "Disclosure is limited to the information the client authorized and the purpose stated. Sending the whole record exceeds the consent and the minimum necessary standard."),
("A 30-year-old client seems unable to understand the risks of treatment after a head injury. The counselor should first:",
 "Assess capacity to consent", ["Proceed with verbal consent", "Ask a friend to sign", "Delay all services a year"],
 "The outline lists assessing competency to provide informed consent. If capacity is lacking, consent comes from a legally authorized person (ACA B.5), not a friend."),
("A client asks to read her own counseling record. Under the ACA Code, the counselor should:",
 "Give access and help her read it", ["Refuse until treatment ends", "Give her only the diagnosis code", "Require a court order first"],
 "ACA B.6.e gives competent clients reasonable access to their records, limited only when there is compelling evidence of serious harm. B.6.f asks counselors to help clients interpret them."),
("An insurer requests psychotherapy notes to approve more sessions. Under HIPAA, these notes:",
 "Need their own authorization", ["Go with any routine claim form", "May be shared by phone", "Must be sent within 30 days"],
 "45 CFR 164.508 requires a specific authorization for psychotherapy notes, kept apart from the rest of the record, and a plan cannot make one a condition of payment."),
("A counselor wants to record sessions for supervision. Before recording, she must:",
 "Ask the client's permission", ["Tell only her supervisor", "Record now, delete it later", "Post a notice in the lobby"],
 "ACA B.6.c requires client permission before recording a session electronically or otherwise. A lobby notice is not informed consent."),
("A client discloses HIV-positive status and refuses to tell his partner. The ACA Code allows the counselor to:",
 "Disclose if law and facts allow", ["Warn the partner right away", "Tell the client's employer first", "Keep it secret in every case"],
 "ACA B.2.c permits disclosure to identifiable third parties at high risk of a contagious, life-threatening disease, after confirming the diagnosis and the client's intent, and only where state law allows."),
("A counselor is moving away and must end work with an ongoing client. Ethically she should:",
 "Give notice, plan a transfer", ["Stop at the very next session", "Mail a short closing letter", "Keep seeing him by text message"],
 "ACA A.11.c and A.11.d call for pretermination counseling and referral, with a transfer planned so care continues. Texting across state lines raises licensure problems."),
("A counselor's personal values clash with a client's same-sex relationship goals. The counselor should:",
 "Keep working; get consultation", ["Refer him for values reasons", "Share her own views openly", "Steer his goals gently toward hers"],
 "ACA A.11.b forbids referral based only on the counselor's personal values. Counselors are expected to bracket their values and seek training or consultation."),
("Before a new group begins, the leader meets each applicant. This step is required to:",
 "Screen for fit with the group", ["Collect the full fee in advance", "Assign seating for session one", "Gather data for research"],
 "ACA A.9.a asks group leaders to screen prospective members so their needs and goals fit the group and they will not impede it."),
("A counselor sees a married couple together. At the start she should clarify:",
 "Who the clients are", ["Which partner is at fault", "That no secrets can exist", "What each partner earns"],
 "ACA A.8 asks counselors who work with more than one person to clarify at the outset who is the client and the nature of the relationship with each person."),
("A client in a rural town is also the counselor's child's teacher. This is an example of:",
 "A dual relationship", ["Countertransference", "A sexual boundary violation", "Client abandonment"],
 "In small communities some nonprofessional contact cannot be avoided. ACA A.6.b asks the counselor to document the reasons, potential benefits and steps taken to prevent harm."),
("A counselor feels drained, dreads sessions and cannot sleep. The first ethical step is to:",
 "Get help and cut her caseload", ["Push through until vacation", "Close her practice at once", "Tell clients about her stress"],
 "ACA C.2.g asks counselors to monitor for impairment and seek help or limit professional duties when impaired. Disclosing her stress to clients shifts the burden onto them."),
("A supervisee in counseling says he dislikes a client and wants to transfer her. The supervisor should first:",
 "Explore his reaction first", ["Approve the transfer", "Report him to the board", "Tell the client about it"],
 "Supervision is the place to examine countertransference. A transfer may follow, but a reaction is first something to understand, not a reason for discipline."),
("In telemental health, the counselor should know the client's physical location at each session because:",
 "Emergency help must reach her", ["Billing codes vary by city", "Video quality depends on it", "It proves the session took place"],
 "ACA Section H asks distance counselors to know the client's location so they can follow emergency procedures, and to follow the laws of the counselor's and the client's locations."),
("A client living in another state wants to keep online sessions after moving. The counselor must check:",
 "Licensure in the client's state", ["The client's internet speed", "Her home state's tax rules", "Whether the client changed jobs"],
 "Counselors practicing at a distance follow the laws of the client's location as well as their own (ACA H.1.b), and practice usually requires authorization where the client is."),
("A judge subpoenas a client's records and the client objects. The counselor should:",
 "Assert privilege, get advice", ["Mail the full chart at once", "Destroy the session notes", "Ignore the subpoena entirely"],
 "Under ACA B.2.d the counselor seeks the client's consent or works to withhold or limit disclosure. A subpoena differs from a court order; legal advice is often needed, and destroying records is never acceptable."),
("A counselor plans a single-case study of her client's progress. She must first:",
 "Obtain informed consent", ["Change the client's name only", "Wait until the case closes", "Ask her supervisor to approve"],
 "Using client information for research needs informed consent, and the outline includes understanding research methods. Disguising identity does not replace consent."),
("A client with a hearing impairment needs to follow the session. The counselor should:",
 "Arrange an interpreter", ["Speak more loudly than usual", "Ask a relative to sign for him", "Refer him to another agency"],
 "The outline lists providing adequate accommodations for clients with disabilities. A qualified interpreter or aid serves the client; a relative interpreting compromises confidentiality."),
("A counselor bills insurance for a family session as individual therapy. This is best described as:",
 "Fraudulent billing", ["A common billing shortcut", "Acceptable with client consent", "A coding preference"],
 "ACA C.6.b requires accurate reporting of services. Billing a service as something it was not is fraud even when the client agrees."),
("At the first session, a counselor reviews fees, cancellation policy and emergency contacts. This is part of:",
 "Informed consent", ["Case conceptualization", "Treatment evaluation", "Clinical supervision"],
 "ACA A.2.b lists fees, billing, the nature of services, confidentiality limits and emergency procedures among the elements of informed consent."),
("A counselor notices a colleague drinking before seeing clients. After raising it with him, she should next:",
 "Consult, then report if needed", ["Post about the concern online", "Tell his clients privately", "Wait for a formal complaint"],
 "ACA I.2.a and I.2.b ask counselors to try an informal resolution first, and to report to the appropriate body when that fails or harm is substantial."),
("A client's 16-year-old daughter is seen for counseling. Her mother demands every detail. The counselor should:",
 "Agree on what will be shared", ["Share everything the mother asks", "Refuse to speak with the mother", "End counseling with the minor"],
 "ACA B.5.b recognizes the legal rights of parents while protecting the minor's confidentiality as far as possible. Agreements about what will be shared are best set at the start."),
("A counselor keeps clinical files on a laptop. To protect them she should:",
 "Encrypt them with a password", ["Store them in a shared folder", "Email copies to herself", "Print backups to keep at home"],
 "ACA B.6.b and H.2.d ask counselors to keep records secure and use encryption for electronic records and communication."),
("An agency wants to use an AI tool that drafts notes from session audio. The counselor should first:",
 "Disclose it and get consent", ["Use it without telling clients", "Ban all technology at the agency", "Save the audio indefinitely"],
 "Recording and using technology in counseling require informed consent (ACA B.6.c and H.2.a), including how data are stored and who can see them."),
("A client brings her counselor a small handmade card at termination. The counselor should most likely:",
 "Accept it graciously", ["Refuse it and explain why", "Pay her for the card", "Report it to the supervisor"],
 "ACA A.10.f asks counselors to weigh the gift's value, the client's motivation and the effect of refusing. A small token at termination is usually accepted; refusing can hurt the relationship."),
("A client asks whether his counselor will testify for him in a custody case. The counselor should:",
 "Explain the role conflict", ["Agree to testify for him", "Write a custody report", "End counseling right away"],
 "ACA D.1.e and E.13 separate the treating role from a forensic evaluation. A treating counselor should not evaluate custody; she can explain the conflict and suggest a separate evaluator."),
("A counselor is unsure whether a situation requires breaking confidentiality. A sound next step is to:",
 "Consult a supervisor", ["Decide alone and move on", "Ask the client to choose", "Disclose it to be safe"],
 "ACA B.2.e and I.1.c encourage consultation when the right course is unclear. Disclosing just to be safe can itself breach confidentiality."),
("Before group therapy, a member asks if other members must keep things private. The leader should explain:",
 "Privacy cannot be guaranteed", ["Members are legally bound to it", "Leaks lead to fines", "Members sign a binding pledge"],
 "ACA B.4.a asks leaders to stress the importance of confidentiality in groups and to tell members that it cannot be guaranteed, since members are not bound as counselors are."),
]
